---
import Portfolio from "../layouts/Portfolio.astro";
---

<Portfolio
  title="How this site ships"
  description="Static Astro pages, a Starlight handbook, and reviewed delivery to private S3 and CloudFront."
>
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Engineering</span
    >
  </nav>
  <div class="case-hero">
    <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">
      A small site.<br />A considered release.
    </h1><p class="lead">
      Static pages, a useful handbook, and a delivery process that keeps content
      and infrastructure changes clear.
    </p>
  </div>
  <article class="article markdown engineering-article">
    <h2 id="architecture">Clear responsibilities</h2>
    <p>
      Astro builds the portfolio from typed project content. Starlight provides
      handbook navigation and code presentation. One Pagefind index searches
      both. The browser loads static files, locally hosted fonts, and small
      scripts for search and the homepage stars.
    </p>
    <ol class="delivery-steps">
      <li>Markdown and profile data</li><li>Checked Astro build</li><li>
        Saved release artifact
      </li><li>Private S3 and CloudFront</li>
    </ol>
    <h2 id="delivery">Build once, publish the tested artifact</h2>
    <p>
      Changes on dev and pull requests are checked before merging to protected
      main. The workflow validates content, types, and generated links, then
      saves the site artifact. The publishing job downloads that artifact,
      assumes a scoped AWS role through GitHub OIDC, and checks the active
      CloudFront Free subscription before uploading.
    </p>
    <p>
      Assets are uploaded before pages. CloudFront is invalidated, then public
      pages and search assets are checked. Previous objects remain available for
      cached pages, and S3 versioning provides a recovery path.
    </p>
    <h2 id="infrastructure">Review infrastructure separately</h2>
    <p>
      The hosting stack is deployed on private S3 and CloudFront with HTTPS and
      Origin Access Control. A separate manual workflow prepares a
      CloudFormation change set. Infrastructure application waits for approval
      of that exact proposal.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Free-only constraint stays in the template and delivery checks. A
      custom domain is a separate future decision.
    </p>
    <h2 id="history">Keep the learning visible</h2>
    <p>
      This project began as Flask and PostgreSQL, then used a Python and Jinja
      static build managed with uv. The portfolio now uses Astro and Starlight.
      The Flask app and container deployment tooling have been retired; their
      source remains in Git history. Python still provides a timed local preview,
      and the existing database data is preserved outside the public static site.
    </p>
    <h2 id="evidence">Inspect the implementation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/Joskingy/Python_Project"
          >Project repository</a
        >
      </li><li>
        <a href="/handbook/aws/github-actions-oidc.html"
          >Workflow identity and AWS permissions</a
        >
      </li><li>
        <a href="/handbook/iac/change-set-review.html"
          >Infrastructure change-set review</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Portfolio>
